- Current monthly income: Lower income might increase stress and risk of depression; higher income might be protective.
- Fear of pregnancy: Experiencing fear during pregnancy may increase risk of postpartum depression.
- Major changes or losses during pregnancy: Such events likely increase risk of postpartum depression.
- Need for Support: Higher need for support likely correlates with postpartum depression.
- Mode of delivery: Caesarean section might have slightly higher risk due to complications or recovery issues than normal delivery.
- Received Support: Higher received support may protect against postpartum depression.
- Relax/sleep when the newborn is asleep: Inability to relax/sleep may increase risk of postpartum depression.
- Relationship with the newborn: Poor or neutral relationship likely correlates with postpartum depression; good or very good correlates negatively.

